WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.967 --> 00:00:02.268
-We're here with Chris Hayes.

00:00:02.268 --> 00:00:04.270
You wrote a fascinating piece
in "The New Yorker"

00:00:04.270 --> 00:00:08.841
about Internet, about celebrity,
about surveillance,

00:00:08.841 --> 00:00:11.277
and just in general
the idea of fame

00:00:11.277 --> 00:00:14.814
and how there's less gateways
to fame than ever before.

00:00:14.814 --> 00:00:16.916
But there's a great price
that is incurred on people.

00:00:16.916 --> 00:00:18.284
Can you speak to your thesis?

00:00:18.284 --> 00:00:21.121
-Yeah, I mean, I think
that what has happened is

00:00:21.121 --> 00:00:24.690
that the experience of having
someone you don't know know you,

00:00:24.690 --> 00:00:27.193
a stranger seeing you,
knowing you, surveilling you,

00:00:27.193 --> 00:00:29.829
is fundamentally alien
and alienating.

00:00:29.829 --> 00:00:31.931
It's fundamentally outside

00:00:31.931 --> 00:00:36.102
the normal portfolio
of human experience, right?

00:00:36.102 --> 00:00:38.571
We're designed to have
kin relationships

00:00:38.571 --> 00:00:41.173
and then maybe
there's some king somewhere

00:00:41.173 --> 00:00:42.442
that we know about, right,

00:00:42.442 --> 00:00:44.777
or there's some god
in the pantheon.

00:00:44.777 --> 00:00:46.846
But to have strangers
know about you,

00:00:46.846 --> 00:00:50.049
which is what social media
has introduced to everyone,

00:00:50.049 --> 00:00:53.085
really psychologically
can be quite toxic.

00:00:53.085 --> 00:00:54.620
And I think -- You know,
I said in the piece

00:00:54.620 --> 00:00:56.355
that in the same way
that electricity

00:00:56.355 --> 00:00:58.324
went from a kind of thing
only the elite had

00:00:58.324 --> 00:00:59.725
to being accessible to everyone,

00:00:59.725 --> 00:01:03.462
I think that experience
of strangers knowing about you,

00:01:03.462 --> 00:01:06.232
responding to you, you know,
saying something about you,

00:01:06.232 --> 00:01:07.433
looking at your picture,

00:01:07.433 --> 00:01:09.268
that used to be
a very small group of people

00:01:09.268 --> 00:01:11.104
had that experience,
people like yourself or me

00:01:11.104 --> 00:01:12.972
or people that were
in the public eye.

00:01:12.972 --> 00:01:14.707
And it's now been
kind of democratized

00:01:14.707 --> 00:01:17.276
in ways that I think
is wreaking real psychological

00:01:17.276 --> 00:01:20.212
and social havoc in ways that
we haven't fully appreciated.

00:01:20.212 --> 00:01:21.613
And you sort of are honest
about the fact

00:01:21.613 --> 00:01:24.183
that you're somebody
who can put something out there

00:01:24.183 --> 00:01:26.152
and one negative comment

00:01:26.152 --> 00:01:29.121
would sort of eradicate
a thousand positive comments.

00:01:29.121 --> 00:01:32.292
-Yeah, and I think that math
is true for a lot of people.

00:01:32.292 --> 00:01:34.460
I mean, maybe
I'm just a snowflake.

00:01:34.460 --> 00:01:35.995
[ Both laugh ]

00:01:35.995 --> 00:01:37.964
But I think that we are --

00:01:37.964 --> 00:01:41.668
Again, we're conditioned to
think about these relationships

00:01:41.668 --> 00:01:45.271
as being the relationships with
the people that we love, right?

00:01:45.271 --> 00:01:47.373
It's like you want the approval
and the affection

00:01:47.373 --> 00:01:49.542
and the respect of people
that you love and respect,

00:01:49.542 --> 00:01:52.678
and then if there's
like some rando

00:01:52.678 --> 00:01:54.280
who says something mean to you,

00:01:54.280 --> 00:01:56.616
you're processing that
psychologically

00:01:56.616 --> 00:01:59.018
as if that was a person
you loved and respected.

00:01:59.018 --> 00:02:01.988
And it takes a lot of willpower
and training, I think,

00:02:01.988 --> 00:02:03.289
psychologically
to get rid of that.

00:02:03.289 --> 00:02:06.426
But I think about being a
teenager under those conditions,

00:02:06.426 --> 00:02:08.928
because that's already the case
of teenage life

00:02:08.928 --> 00:02:11.697
is you're very worried about
people's perception of you.

00:02:11.697 --> 00:02:13.165
You're very much
forming your identity.

00:02:13.165 --> 00:02:15.468
And then it's like
everyone in the world

00:02:15.468 --> 00:02:18.404
can see you at that moment.

00:02:18.404 --> 00:02:19.806
There's something
happening there

00:02:19.806 --> 00:02:21.274
that I think
is pretty unnerving.

00:02:21.274 --> 00:02:23.476
-With that said, you wrote
this piece for "The New Yorker."

00:02:23.476 --> 00:02:24.510
You put it out in the world.

00:02:24.510 --> 00:02:26.111
Did you immediately look
to read comments?

00:02:26.111 --> 00:02:27.346
-Oh, absolutely.
Yeah, yeah.

00:02:27.346 --> 00:02:29.648
I was just, like, following.
I was just like --

00:02:29.648 --> 00:02:32.751
I was just like a junkie
looking for the dopamine hit.

00:02:32.751 --> 00:02:35.154
Like, "Yes, yes, yes."

00:02:35.154 --> 00:02:37.556
Approval of strangers.
They like the piece.

00:02:37.556 --> 00:02:39.225
-Well, I know
I'm not a stranger,

00:02:39.225 --> 00:02:40.626
but I like the piece
a great deal, as well.

00:02:40.626 --> 00:02:43.029
And it's always a pleasure
having you come upstairs.

00:02:43.029 --> 00:02:44.363
Thanks for being back.
-Thanks.

00:02:44.363 --> 00:02:45.664
-Chris Hayes, everybody.

00:02:45.664 --> 00:02:48.067
"All In" airs weeknights
at 8:00 on MSNBC.

00:02:48.067 --> 00:02:50.235
You can listen to
"Why Is This Happening?"

00:02:50.235 --> 00:02:51.604
wherever you get your podcasts.

